-
-/* --- Overlay / surface structure -------------------------------------- */
-
-.fos-overlay {
-  position: relative;
-  overflow: visible;
-}
-
-.fos-overlay.fos-overlay-clip {
-  overflow: hidden;
-}
-
-/**
- * A Stack is a containing block too.
- *
- * `place` is not exclusive to Overlay children. Figma treats an absolutely
- * positioned child as an exception INSIDE an auto-layout frame, not as an
- * abandonment of it, so the compiler keeps such a frame a Stack and puts
- * `place` on the one child Figma placed — see `classify` in @fanos/compile,
- * which argues the case at length and measured what demoting the frame to an
- * Overlay costs.
- *
- * That intent only survives if the Stack is positioned. Without this rule the
- * placed child resolves its insets against the nearest positioned ANCESTOR,
- * which for a row nested six frames deep is the page root: the fan-preferences
- * player rows each hold an absolutely-placed plate and a remove button, and all
- * eight of them flew out of their cards and stacked up against the right edge
- * of the document.
- *
- * `relative` with no offsets moves nothing and paints nothing. It establishes a
- * containing block, and a stacking context only for a box that sets `z-index`
- * — which is what `place.z` means anyway.
- */
-.fos-stack {
-  position: relative;
-}
-
-/**
- * Absolute fill box. Token surface paint lives on the CHILD so tokens can keep
- * `position: relative` for inset ::before borders without collapsing this box.
- */
-.fos-surface-layer {
-  position: absolute;
-  inset: 0;
-  z-index: 0;
-  overflow: hidden;
-  pointer-events: none;
-}
-
-.fos-surface-layer > [class*="fos-surface-"] {
-  width: 100%;
-  height: 100%;
-  box-sizing: border-box;
-}
-
-/* Anchored children above the surface; stacking is DOM order among peers. */
-.fos-overlay > .fos-node {
-  z-index: 1;
-}
-
-/**
- * A z-index makes a stacking context, and a node blends only with what is in its
- * own: a blended picture two Overlays down would mix with nothing. So on the way
- * to a blended node the z-index goes. Positioned, the children still paint over
- * the surface layer and in DOM order, which is all it was there for.
- */
-.fos-overlay:has(.fos-blend) > .fos-node {
-  z-index: auto;
-}
-
-/**
- * The other half of that. An Overlay with `z-index: auto` (because its parent
- * holds a blend) makes no stacking context, so its own children's `z-index: 1`
- * and its surface layer's `z-index: 0` leak into the parent's and paint over
- * every later sibling: a full-section mask picture in one Overlay dimmed the
- * heading and toggle of the next. So an Overlay that sits directly in one that
- * holds a blend, and has nothing blended inside itself, closes its own context.
- * Only there: a frame with no blend never had the rule above, and its Overlays
- * keep the stacking they always had.
- */
-.fos-overlay:has(.fos-blend) > .fos-overlay:not(:has(.fos-blend)) {
-  isolation: isolate;
-}
-
-/* --- Image + scrim ---------------------------------------------------- */
-
-.fos-image {
-  display: block;
-}
-
-/* Only non-anchored images establish a containing block for the scrim. */
-.fos-image:not([data-fos-anchored]) {
-  position: relative;
-  overflow: hidden;
-}
-
-.fos-image[data-fos-anchored] {
-  /* position comes from place.anchor (absolute); allow cutout bleed */
-  overflow: visible;
-}
-
-.fos-image img {
-  display: block;
-  width: 100%;
-  height: 100%;
-}
-
-.fos-image[data-scrim]::after {
-  content: "";
-  position: absolute;
-  inset: 0;
-  pointer-events: none;
-  background-image: var(--fos-image-scrim);
-}
-
-/* Anchored images need a positioned wrapper for the scrim pseudo. */
-.fos-image[data-fos-anchored][data-scrim] {
-  /* create containing block without fighting absolute placement from place */
-}
-.fos-image[data-fos-anchored][data-scrim]::after {
-  /* still works: absolute positioned element is containing block for ::after */
-}
-
-/* --- Divider ---------------------------------------------------------- */
-
-.fos-divider-horizontal {
-  height: 1px;
-  width: 100%;
-  border: 0;
-  padding: 0;
-  margin: 0;
-}
-
-.fos-divider-vertical {
-  width: 1px;
-  align-self: stretch;
-  border: 0;
-  padding: 0;
-  margin: 0;
-}
-
-/* --- Text orientation -------------------------------------------------
- * These sit on an INNER span, never on the anchored element: `inset-inline-*`
- * resolves against an element's own writing mode, so putting `vertical-rl` on
- * the positioned box swaps its axes and `mid-start` lands top-centre.
- * `inline-block` is required for writing-mode and transform to take effect.
- */
-
-.fos-text-vertical-up,
-.fos-text-vertical-down {
-  display: inline-block;
-  writing-mode: vertical-rl;
-}
-
-/* vertical-rl reads top-to-bottom; the half-turn makes it read bottom-to-top. */
-.fos-text-vertical-up {
-  transform: rotate(180deg);
-}
-
-/* --- Text truncation -------------------------------------------------
- * Line clamp, not character truncation: the design clips by line box, and the
- * line count is what the IR reports (`text.lines`).
- */
-
-.fos-text-clamp {
-  display: -webkit-box;
-  -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
-  overflow: hidden;
-}
-
-/* --- Button ------------------------------------------------------------
- *
- * One rule set for every variant. `Button.tsx` resolves which token family
- * applies and hands the values down as custom properties, so adding a variant
- * to the theme needs no CSS here.
- *
- * Every fallback is `transparent` or `inherit` rather than a colour, because a
- * family legitimately omits parts: `link` has no surface, `filled` has no
- * border. A missing var must disappear, not paint black.
- */
-
-.fos-button {
-  display: inline-flex;
-  align-items: center;
-  justify-content: center;
-  gap: var(--fos-space-1, 0.25rem);
-  /* A button is a control, not text: it never wraps mid-label by default. */
-  white-space: nowrap;
-  text-decoration: none;
-  cursor: pointer;
-  border: 0;
-  border-radius: var(--fos-radius-md, 0);
-  background: var(--fos-btn-surface, transparent);
-  color: var(--fos-btn-text, inherit);
-  /**
-   * No font declarations here, deliberately.
-   *
-   * `<button>` does come with a UA font that has to be overridden — but the
-   * `fos-type-*` class Button.tsx applies is what overrides it, and these rules
-   * are emitted AFTER the theme's type rules with the same specificity. A
-   * `font: inherit` reset here therefore wins the cascade and silently wipes
-   * the token: the news section's VIEW MORE label rendered in the UA serif with
-   * the type class present and losing.
-   */
-  transition: background-color 120ms ease, color 120ms ease, border-color 120ms ease;
-}
-
-.fos-button-outline {
-  border: 1px solid var(--fos-btn-border, currentColor);
-}
-
-.fos-button-link,
-.fos-button-no-padding {
-  background: none;
-}
-
-.fos-button-link {
-  text-decoration: underline;
-  text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
-}
-
-/* `:hover` only where the pointer can actually hover, so a tap does not stick. */
-@media (hover: hover) {
-  .fos-button:hover:not(:disabled):not([aria-disabled="true"]) {
-    background: var(--fos-btn-surface-hover, var(--fos-btn-surface, transparent));
-    color: var(--fos-btn-text-hover, var(--fos-btn-text, inherit));
-    border-color: var(--fos-btn-border-hover, var(--fos-btn-border, currentColor));
-  }
-}
-
-/**
- * Focus is not optional. A design that never drew a focus state still has to
- * be keyboard-operable, so this is the SDK's floor rather than a token read.
- */
-.fos-button:focus-visible {
-  outline: 2px solid currentColor;
-  outline-offset: 2px;
-}
-
-.fos-button:disabled,
-.fos-button[aria-disabled="true"] {
-  background: var(--fos-btn-surface-disabled, var(--fos-btn-surface, transparent));
-  color: var(--fos-btn-text-disabled, var(--fos-btn-text, inherit));
-  border-color: var(--fos-btn-border-disabled, var(--fos-btn-border, currentColor));
-  cursor: not-allowed;
-}
-
-/* Mid-flight: the label stays put, so the control does not resize under a tap. */
-.fos-button[aria-busy="true"] {
-  cursor: progress;
-}
-
-/* --- Icon placeholder ------------------------------------------------- */
-
-.fos-icon-unknown {
-  display: inline-flex;
-  align-items: center;
-  justify-content: center;
-  opacity: 0.5;
-}
-
-/* Preserve newlines in names like SOPHIE\nMOLINEUX */
-.fos-text {
-  /* Authored newlines are meaningful — the design breaks "Ben / McKinney". */
-  white-space: pre-line;
-  /**
-   * `as: h1..h6` and `as: p` bring UA margins (36px for h3 in Chromium). On an
-   * absolutely-positioned Overlay child that silently shifts the node a whole
-   * line off its authored offset and pushes later lines under whatever sits
-   * below it. Semantics must not move pixels.
-   */
-  margin: 0;
-
-  /**
-   * Leading trim — the single biggest source of vertical drift between Figma
-   * and CSS, and the reason a `space.*` gap read straight out of the IR did not
-   * reproduce the design.
-   *
-   * Figma reports a TEXT node's height as roughly
-   *   (lines - 1) * lineHeight + capHeight
-   * — measured on 1:5095: 2 lines of dp_4 (32/34) is 53px, not the 68px CSS
-   * gives. CSS wraps every line in a full `line-height` box, so half the
-   * leading hangs above the first cap and below the last baseline. On a 2-line
-   * heading that is ~13px of phantom space, and it lands *between* the heading
-   * and whatever the design put a 16px gap in front of.
-   *
-   * `trim-both cap alphabetic` makes the box hug cap-height to baseline, which
-   * is exactly Figma's box. Inter-line spacing is untouched — only the outer
-   * edges are trimmed — so a gap token now means the same thing in both tools.
-   */
-  text-box: trim-both cap alphabetic;
-}
-
-/**
- * …but never on clamped text.
- *
- * Line clamping implies `overflow: hidden`, so a cap-height top edge SHEARS
- * every ascender and i-dot on the first line, and an alphabetic bottom edge
- * shears the descenders on the last. Unclamped text overflows its box
- * harmlessly; clamped text does not.
- *
- * Trim buys nothing here anyway: a truncating Figma node carries an explicit
- * height (`I1:5131;10090:3705` is a fixed 43px), so the box comes from
- * `size.h`, not from the leading.
- *
- * Must stay AFTER `.fos-text` — equal specificity, so source order decides.
- */
-.fos-text-clamp,
-.fos-text-has-vertical,
-.fos-text-untrimmed {
-  text-box: normal;
-}
-
-/* Figma auto-width text: one line, spaces as authored. */
-.fos-text-nowrap {
-  white-space: pre;
-}
-
-/**
- * …but a clamped node still has to OCCUPY the ink box.
- *
- * The exclusion above is right about shearing: clamping implies
- * `overflow: hidden`, so a cap-height top edge cuts every ascender on the first
- * line and an alphabetic bottom edge cuts the descenders on the last. Rendered
- * with `text-box: trim-both` the news grid's summaries visibly lost the tops of
- * their J, V and C and the tails of their g, p and y.
- *
- * What was wrong was the second half of the argument — that trim buys nothing
- * here because a truncating Figma node carries an explicit height. That is true
- * of `I1:5131;10090:3705` and false of this design: every clamped text on the
- * news grid is `autoResize: HEIGHT`, so the DSL says `size.h: "auto"` and the
- * box comes from the leading after all. Left untrimmed they stood 10px taller
- * than Figma each, 20px per card, and the lead card's column clipped.
- *
- * So: keep the full line boxes — nothing is clipped, nothing is sheared — and
- * pull the outer edges in with margin instead. The element still RENDERS at
- * `lines × line-height`; it only OCCUPIES `(lines - 1) × line-height + cap`,
- * which is Figma's box. `1cap` and `1lh` are the browser's own metrics for the
- * element's actual font, so this needs no per-typeface table and stays correct
- * when the type token changes.
- *
- * Half the leading per edge: `(1cap - 1lh) / 2` is negative whenever cap height
- * is below the line height, which is every text style in the system.
- */
-.fos-text-clamp {
-  margin-block: calc((1cap - 1lh) / 2);
-}
-
-/**
- * Same pull on the top edge, so the ink still starts at the box's top; an equal
- * and opposite push on the bottom, so the footprint stays the DECLARED height
- * instead of losing the leading a second time. Occupied = h − half + half = h.
- */
-.fos-text-clamp.fos-text-fixed-h {
-  margin-block: calc((1cap - 1lh) / 2) calc((1lh - 1cap) / 2);
-}
